.TH TINYGL 2
.SH NAME
tglinit, tglresize, tglread, tglclose \- classic OpenGL subset over GPU files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <GL/gl.h>
.br
.B #include <tinygl.h>
.PP
.B
void tglinit(char *device, int width, int height)
.PP
.B
void tglresize(int width, int height)
.PP
.B
void tglread(void *pixels, int bytes)
.PP
.B
void tglclose(void)
.SH DESCRIPTION
TinyGL implements a subset of classic OpenGL using native Plan 9 C.
Matrix transformations and vertex lighting run on the CPU.
Clipping, interpolation, rasterization and depth testing use VirGL through
.IR gpu (3).
No Mesa, GLX, external compiler or Linux emulation is required in the guest.
.PP
.I Tglinit
creates the process's single current context and an off-screen color/depth
target of the requested size.
.I Device
is the directory containing the GPU's
.B clone
and
.B caps
files; nil selects
.BR #∇ .
The directory can be bound elsewhere in the caller's namespace.
Both dimensions must be between 1 and 2048.
The initial viewport covers the target.
.PP
The supported GL operations include immediate points, lines, triangles,
strips, fans, quads and convex polygons; float vertex/color/normal arrays
through
.BR glArrayElement ;
flat and smooth colors; matrix stacks, transformations and perspective;
face culling; one-sided vertex lighting and materials; display lists;
and clearing color and depth.
Depth testing uses LESS and writes depth while enabled.
Points and lines have size one. Polygons are filled.
The header declares the available entry points, not a complete GL API.
.PP
Float array strides are byte counts; zero means tightly packed.
Display lists own the values obtained by
.BR glArrayElement ,
not pointers to client arrays.
List names are in the range 1 through 1023 and nesting is limited to 64.
Generated names are reserved but remain undefined until
.BR glEndList .
Redefinition preserves the old list until compilation finishes.
Calls to undefined lists do nothing.
.PP
.I Tglresize
finishes pending drawing and replaces the render target, releasing the old
resources. Its contents become undefined.
It does not change GL state or the viewport: call
.B glViewport
as appropriate for the new size.
Viewport dimensions may be zero and are limited to 4096; coordinates are
limited to the range -4096 through 4096.
.PP
.I Tglread
finishes pending drawing, downloads the color target and copies it into
.IR pixels .
.I Bytes
must be at least width times height times four.
Pixels are tightly packed, top row first, in the XRGB32 byte layout used by
.IR draw (2).
GL window coordinates still have their origin at the lower left.
The application owns the buffer and chooses how to display or save it.
.PP
.B glFlush
submits pending primitives and waits for completion.
Batches also finish when full or when incompatible drawing state changes.
.I Tglclose
finishes pending drawing, closes the GPU context and frees its resources,
display lists, matrix stacks and other CPU allocations.
A subsequent
.I tglinit
creates fresh state.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
uchar pixels[64*64*4];

tglinit(nil, 64, 64);
glClearColor(0, 0, 0, 1);
glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT | GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT);
glBegin(GL_TRIANGLES);
glColor3f(1, 0, 0); glVertex2f(-.8, -.8);
glColor3f(0, 1, 0); glVertex2f(.8, -.8);
glColor3f(0, 0, 1); glVertex2f(0, .8);
glEnd();
tglread(pixels, sizeof pixels);
tglclose();
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libtinygl
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR glgears (1),
